<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bay of Naples</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulf of Naples</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> is a gulf on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, overlooked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a> and closed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a> peninsula and the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a> and Capri. Greek colonists settled its shores from the 8th century BCE, founding Cumae and Neapolis, and it later became a favoured resort of wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples.jpg" alt="Bay of Naples" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Wolfgang Moroder. Feel free to use my photos, but please mention me as the author and send me a message. … · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naples_from_the_Castello_Sant_Elmo_with_Abbazia_San_Martino_the_port_and_the_Vesuv.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, known in Italian as the Golfo di Napoli, is a stretch of coastline about 15 kilometres wide on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a>, facing the Metropolitan City of Naples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>. It opens westward into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a>. Naples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pozzuoli">Pozzuoli</a> lie on its northern shore, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a> on the east, and the Sorrento Peninsula and the town of Sorrento on the south. The peninsula separates the gulf from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-salerno">Gulf of Salerno</a>, which includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalfi-coast">Amalfi Coast</a>. The islands of Capri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a> and Procida lie within it, and local waters are home to fin and sperm whales and to dolphins. The area is a tourist destination, and the gulf hosted the sailing events of the 1960 Summer Olympics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Roman ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herculaneum">Herculaneum</a> stand on the northern coast at the foot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a>, which destroyed them in the eruption of 79 CE. Another tale from Roman times concerns the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>, who is said to have built a bridge of boats across the bay and to have ridden over it in the armour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>.</p><p>The Roman biographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> describes the work in The Twelve Caesars. According to him, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> had trading vessels gathered from all quarters and moored in two rows, with earth spread over them in the manner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Appian Way</a>, to make a bridge of about three and a half miles from Baiae to the mole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pozzuoli">Puteoli</a>. He crossed it and recrossed it for two days, on the first mounted on a horse and on the second in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, with a young Parthian hostage named Darius and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohort</a> of the Praetorian guards in attendance.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> reports several explanations. Most people thought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> was imitating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>, who bridged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, and others thought he meant to frighten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which he was about to invade. Suetonius himself repeats his grandfather's account of what courtiers close to Caligula said. The astrologer Thrasyllus had told the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> that Caligula had no more chance of becoming emperor than of riding on horseback across the gulf of Baiae.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gulf of Naples” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…won a series of victories against the Angevins after Peter III took Sicily in 1282: off Malta in 1283, in the <b>Bay of Naples</b> in 1284, where Charles of Salerno was captured, and at Les Formigues in 1285, which wrecked the French invasion known as the Aragonese Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The words celebrate Borgo Santa Lucia, a picturesque waterfront district on the <b>Gulf of Naples</b>, in the voice of a boatman inviting passers-by to take a ride in his boat and enjoy the cool of the evening.</blockquote><a href="/songs/santa-lucia">Santa Lucia</a></li><li><blockquote>A strong earthquake badly damaged towns around the <b>Bay of Naples</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-5">5 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of the Sicilian Vespers, an Aragonese–Sicilian fleet under Roger of Lauria defeated an Angevin fleet in the <b>Gulf of Naples</b> and captured its commander, Prince Charles of Salerno.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-5">5 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Euboean Greeks from Chalcis and Eretria founded Cumae around 740 BCE on the Campanian coast north of the <b>Bay of Naples</b>, having first settled the island of Pithekoussai (Ischia).</blockquote><a href="/state/cumae">Cumae</a></li><li><blockquote>Sorrento, an old Roman town on the peninsula south of the <b>Bay of Naples</b>, was part of the Byzantine Duchy of Naples.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-sorrento">Duchy of Sorrento</a></li></ul></section>
